  "final_answer": "I now have a definitive answer. Based on my research:\n\n## Answer\n\nThe software requirement for iPhones (and iPads) to support **AirDrop's ability to continue transmitting over the internet when out of Bluetooth/Wi-Fi range** is:\n\n**iOS 17.1 or later**\n\nThis feature — where AirDrop transfers can continue over the internet even after devices move out of Bluetooth and Wi-Fi range — was introduced in **iOS 17.1** (it was NOT included in the original iOS 17.0 release).\n\n### Supporting Requirements (both iPhones must meet these):\n- **iOS version**: iOS 17.1 or later (the minimum version that introduced the internet-continuation feature)\n- **Compatible devices**: Both devices must be AirDrop-capable Apple devices (iPhone, iPad, or Mac)\n- **Wireless connections**: Both Wi-Fi and Bluetooth must be enabled on both devices\n- **Initial proximity**: Devices must be within ~30 feet (10 meters) to start the transfer\n- **Internet access**: Both devices need internet connectivity for the transfer to continue once they separate\n- **Personal Hotspot**: Must be disconnected on either device\n- **VPN**: Should not block local networking\n\n### Compatible iPhone Models:\nSince iOS 17.1 is the minimum, the iPhones that support this feature are those compatible with iOS 17, which includes **iPhone XS, iPhone XS Max, iPhone XR, and later models** (2018 onwards).\n\nSources: Apple Support (Use AirDrop to send items to nearby Apple devices) and iTechGuides (\"How to Use AirDrop Over the Internet On iPhone in iOS 17\").",
